# AGENTS.md

Guide for AI coding agents working in `{{.Name}}`, a [Forge](https://github.com/rocketinsidecorp/forge-go)
application in Go{{if .DB}} on {{.DB.Title}}{{end}}{{if .API}}, API-only (JSON, no views){{end}}. README.md has the commands
to run it; this file is what to know before changing it. Prefer the smallest change that solves the task.

## Layout and dependency rules

Imports point inward. `app/architecture_test.go` enforces this with the standard library and
fails with the file, the line and the forbidden import:

| Directory | Holds | May import |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `app/domain` | entities, value objects, rules, errors | itself, the standard library without `net/http`, `database/sql`, `os`, Forge's `uuid` and `fault` |
| `app/application` | use cases and the ports they need | `app/domain`, itself, Forge's `uuid`, `fault`, `pagination`, `tenancy` |
| `app/adapters/*` | HTTP handlers, persistence, pages | the inner rings and Forge; never `app/bootstrap` |
| `app/bootstrap` | the composition root: wires everything | anything; the only place that composes |

`app/jobs`, `app/mailers`{{if not .API}}, `app/views`{{end}}, `db` and `config` sit outside the rings and never import
`app/bootstrap`. `cmd/{{.Name}}` is `main`. Do not weaken the architecture test to make code compile: move the
code to the right ring. A new third-party package for the inner rings goes in `extraAllowedImports`, in a
reviewed commit.

## Commands

Run from the application directory. `forge help --json` lists every command, flag and exit code.

```sh
forge new NAME [-d DB]                    # a new application (already done here)
{{- if and .API .DB}}
forge generate resource|model|controller|mailer|job NAME ...   # g is short; add --dry-run first (controller --api)
{{- else if .API}}
forge generate controller|mailer|job NAME ...   # g is short; add --dry-run first (controller --api)
{{- else if .DB}}
forge generate scaffold|resource|model|controller|mailer|job|view NAME ...   # g is short; add --dry-run first
{{- else}}
forge generate controller|mailer|job|view NAME ...   # g is short; add --dry-run first
{{- end}}
forge destroy KIND NAME ...               # d is short; removes exactly what the generator wrote, unless edited
{{- if .DB}}
forge migrate [status]                    # apply pending migrations; forge rollback reverts the latest
forge seed                                # db.Seed, idempotent
{{- end}}
forge dev                                 # run with rebuild and browser reload
forge test [./pkg ...]                    # go test with .env.development loaded (default ./...)
forge check                               # go build ./... and go vet ./...
forge format --check                      # gofmt; without --check it rewrites
forge lint                                # golangci-lint run ./...
forge build                               # static binary in bin/
forge routes                              # every route of the composed application
```

Generator arguments are `name:type` with `string`, `text`, `int`, `bool`, `time` or `uuid`; a trailing `?` makes
the field optional (quote it: `'summary:text?'`). Money is an integer in minor units, never a float.
{{- if .DB}}

Models, resources and scaffolds need exactly one of `--tenant-scoped` (rows belong to one tenant) or `--global`
(shared by all): decide by who owns the data, never by convenience.
{{- end}}

## Routes seam

Generators mount their routes in `app/bootstrap/routes_gen.go`, in blocks that start with `// forge:`. Never edit
that file by hand: it carries a checksum, the generators refuse to touch it after a hand edit, and `forge destroy`
removes their blocks. Change `app/bootstrap/bootstrap.go` for your own wiring. To replace a generated guard
function, copy it out of `routes_gen.go` into another file of package `bootstrap`, change it there and run any
`forge generate` command again; until then the build fails with "redeclared", never silently.

## Security defaults

- Generated routes are never public by accident: they need the permission guard, or answer 403 until you wire one
  (`--public` exists only for `--global` in applications without Forge identity, for prototypes).
{{- if not .API}}
- Forms carry a CSRF token (`csrfField`) and unsafe browser requests without it are rejected; JSON calls that
  authenticate with the session cookie send it in `X-CSRF-Token`. Templates escape HTML automatically: never
  build HTML by string concatenation.
{{- end}}
{{- if .DB}}
- Tenant-scoped repositories take the tenant from `tenancy.Require(ctx)` and bind `tenant_id` in every statement;
  never read a tenant id from the request body or a query string.
{{- if .DB.Identity}}
- Sign-in, sign-out, two-factor and password-reset pages come with the application. PostgreSQL row-level security
  backs the tenant predicates: the application serves as `app_runtime` and migrates as `app_migrator`
  (`.env.development` and compose set both); do not point it at a superuser.
{{- end}}
- Identifiers are UUIDv7 generated in Go; migrations use no database UUID generator and a released migration is never edited.
{{- end}}
- Never log, print, commit or paste secrets. `.env.development` is committed and holds none; put keys in `.env.local`
  or in `config/credentials/*.json.enc` (edit with `forge credentials edit`; `*.key` files are git-ignored).
  Use `config.Secret` for new secret settings so it prints redacted.

## Tests

```sh
forge test                                # everything, including the architecture test
forge test ./app/ -run TestArchitecture   # only the architecture test
forge format --check && forge check && forge lint   # what CI also runs
```
{{- if and .DB .DB.Server}}

Database tests need the server from `compose.yaml` (`docker compose up -d --wait {{.DB.Service}}`) and skip without
`FORGE_TEST_DATABASE_URL`, which `.env.development` sets; `FORGE_TEST_REQUIRE_DATABASE=true` turns the skip into a
failure. A skipped database test is not a pass.
{{- end}}

Every generator writes its own tests next to the code; keep them passing and add a test with each behavior change.
Do not delete or weaken a test to get green output. Never claim a check passed unless it ran.
